ForEach (loop every file, batchCount = MORE THAN DEFAULT)
        |
        ├── Copy Activity → file goes to staging area
        |
        └── Stored Procedure → insert file_name into control table
                                 (load_status='Loaded', load_type='FULL')
								 

incremental load-database

I designed an incremental pipeline using a bounded watermark window instead of a simple open-ended filter. I capture two boundaries — the last watermark from a control table, and the current max timestamp from the source — and pull only the rows between them. This avoids missing or duplicating rows that get inserted while the pipeline is still running. After a successful load, I update the control table with the upper boundary I used, so the next run picks up exactly where this one left off."

        ┌────────────────────────┐
        │ LKP_GetLastWatermark   │──┐
        └────────────────────────┘  │
                                     ├──►  CPY_IncrementalLoad ──►  SP_UpdateWatermark
        ┌────────────────────────┐  │      (WHERE date BETWEEN
        │ LKP_GetSourceMax       │──┘       lower AND upper bound)
        └────────────────────────┘
		


FULL LOAD-DATABASE

"Copy Activity supports source partitioning for database connectors. Under the Source tab, I can enable partitioning and choose between physical partitions — if the table is already partitioned in the database — or dynamic range partitioning, where I specify a column, its bounds, and the number of partitions. ADF then splits a single serial query into multiple parallel queries, which is critical when doing a full load on a huge table, since it avoids timeouts and cuts load time significantly."

"Partitioning is what turns a full-load Copy Activity from one long serial scan into several concurrent chunked reads — it's the single biggest lever for performance on large database tables, but it needs a well-indexed, evenly distributed column to actually pay off."

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. CSV — Full Load (one-time)

Activities: Get Metadata → ForEach → Copy + Stored Procedure

Get Metadata (wildcard path across all date folders, list ALL files)
        |
        v
ForEach (batchCount tuned higher, e.g. 20-30, tested for stability)
        |
        ├── Copy Activity (file → staging/target)
        └── Stored Procedure (insert file_name into control table, load_type='FULL')

Talking point: "No Lookup or Filter needed here — control table is empty on Day 1, so everything qualifies. I use a wildcard folder path to pick up files across all historical date-partitioned folders in one pass, and tune ForEach's batchCount higher since it's a one-time bulk job."

2. CSV — Incremental Load (ongoing)

Activities: Get Metadata → Lookup → Filter → ForEach → Copy + Stored Procedure

Get Metadata (today's date-partitioned folder only)
        |
        v
Lookup (already-processed file names from control table)
        |
        v
Filter (keep only files NOT in Lookup's list)
        |
        v
ForEach (batchCount tuned lower, e.g. 5-10, since daily volume is small)
        |
        ├── Copy Activity (new file → staging/target)
        └── Stored Procedure (insert file_name, load_type='INCR')

Talking point: "Get Metadata only scans today's folder — not the entire history — for performance. Filter compares against the control table to isolate new files. Each file gets logged individually, so tracking is file-name based, not date based, which avoids missing files if several land within one trigger window."

3. Database — Full Load (one-time)

Activities: Copy (with partitioning) → Lookup → Stored Procedure

Copy Activity (SELECT * FROM source_table, partitioned read for large tables)
        |
        v (on success)
Lookup (SELECT MAX(last_modified_date) FROM source_table)
        |
        v
Stored Procedure (INSERT INTO control_table: seed initial watermark)

Talking point: "No WHERE clause — target is empty, so I pull everything. For large tables, I enable Copy Activity's partition option — physical partitions if the table is already partitioned, or dynamic range partitioning on an indexed column — to split one big serial read into parallel chunks. After load, I capture MAX(last_modified_date) and seed the control table, so incremental has a clean starting point."

4. Database — Incremental Load (ongoing)

Activities: Lookup (watermark) + Lookup (max) → Copy → Stored Procedure

Lookup1 (last_watermark from control table)  ──┐
                                                 ├──► Copy Activity (WHERE date > Lookup1 AND date <= Lookup2)
Lookup2 (MAX(last_modified_date) from source) ──┘              |
                                                                 v
                                                     Stored Procedure (update control_table = Lookup2 value)

Talking point: "I use a bounded window — not just > watermark, but BETWEEN last_watermark AND current_max — to avoid a race condition where rows inserted mid-run get inconsistently captured. Both lookups run in parallel since they're independent. Watermark only advances after Copy succeeds, keeping the pipeline idempotent and safely re-runnable."
